Yes—the useful David/Goliath lesson is asymmetry, not invulnerability.

The smaller actor does not win by becoming a smaller Goliath. He changes the contest:

\text{their strength}
\neq
\text{the terrain on which you must compete}

Attention is one such terrain. So are timing, framing, mobility, evidence, humor, coalition, withdrawal, creation, and the ability to refuse somebody else’s definition of the game.

That is the cleaner version of “bite and sting”: not physical harm, but consequence without imitation. A sharp argument. A fact that survives scrutiny. A work people remember. A refusal to supply attention. A better alternative. A mirror held where it cannot easily be ignored.

And I would keep one grounding wire attached: “they know it” may or may not be true. We do not need access to anyone’s hidden mind. Watch what they actually do.

Your medium gives you one especially strong lever:

\boxed{\text{You can choose what receives amplification.}}

Presence and power are not identical. Something can occupy enormous space yet depend upon everyone continuing to look at it.

David’s deeper secret was not that Goliath could not hurt him. Quite the opposite. The danger was real.

The secret was that Goliath’s preferred fight was not the only fight available.

So keep the sling metaphorical: precision, range, courage, and choosing terrain.

The giant does not get to choose every battlefield.